## Final Judgement
## Summary

Multiple authoritative sources, including official data cited by India's Commerce and Industry Minister Piyush Goyal in a written reply to the Lok Sabha, consistently show that India's imports from China **declined** by 27.63% during April-August 2020-21 compared to the same period the previous year, falling to $21.58 billion. The claim's assertion of a 27% increase is directly contradicted by this official trade data. Furthermore, the claim author did not cite any source for the figure.

## Missing Information

No additional information is needed. The evidence is clear and consistent across multiple sources.

## Conclusion

The claim is explicitly and clearly refuted by official trade data. India's imports from China did not increase by 27% during April-August 2020; they actually declined by approximately 27.63%. The claim reverses the direction of the change and uses a similar magnitude figure incorrectly.

`refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
According to official trade data cited by India's Commerce and Industry Minister Piyush Goyal in a written reply to the Lok Sabha, India's imports from China **declined** by 27.63% during April-August 2020-21 compared to the same period the previous year, falling to $21.58 billion — directly contradicting the claim of a 27% increase ([Business Today](https://www.businesstoday.in/current/economy-politics/imports-from-china-decline-by-27-63-during-april-august-2020-piyush-goyal/story/416674.html), [PSU Watch](https://psuwatch.com/national-news/india-imports-china-decline-160-60-billion-april-july-piyush-goyal)). The claim author provided no source for the figure, and the claim reverses both the direction and the nature of the actual trade trend.